{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792504301,"nonce":"X0FNjbEJ1ptEt5GgCd20m+ALQnA=","message":"Implemented in f4bf83fa872e8077bdf8a1d1e8588a3bbebc9182.\n\nThe reproducer failed before the change: lowering a library containing generic identity and second without local instantiations returned no HIR template for either export. Snapshot packaging then stripped the typed bodies as expected, leaving importing callers without an implementation.\n\nLibrary monomorphization now moves the original templates through the shared specialization context and returns them without deep-copying their bodies. Snapshot optimization temporarily removes those templates from the executable pipeline and restores them unoptimized for split encoding. Whole-program monomorphization still replaces generic calls with cached concrete instances and removes the templates. A missing template body is now an analysis error, and new instance names avoid existing concrete library instances.\n\nDeferred linking decodes reached generic bodies before specialization. Concrete dependencies remain deferred, and the ordinary adoption closure follows template calls and private literals. No snapshot schema change or whole-image decoding was needed.\n\nRegression coverage:\n- A library with no local instantiations retains identity and typed-array reader bodies through snapshot serialization and ID relocation.\n- Fresh importing programs run nested and recursive generic calls, returned array aliases, push, private static strings, and a deferred concrete helper with automatic release and no collector.\n- Interpreter-first execution, Cranelift, LLVM, and mixed native/interpreter calls agree.\n- Native integer, float, boolean and string-pointer array reads use nonzero-index typed GEPs; boolean loads retain alignment 1. Repeated integer type arguments share a single specialization.\n- Existing concrete library specializations do not consume templates or collide with subsequently generated names. A signature-only generic cannot be executed as a specialization.\n- The allocation-audit regression includes an unused generic containing 65536 extra values. Closed-program lowering decodes exactly through and second, and decoding requests less than one eighth of the encoded image size. The unused body remains encoded.\n\nValidation: 49 TypeScript tests pass with default features and with LLVM/allocation-audit enabled, including the pinned conformance acceptance/output checks. The final snapshot regressions also pass with LLVM enabled, and the ignored allocation profile was run explicitly and passed. Shared monomorphization (11), prelowered linking (6), snapshot (13), import-chain (4), and borrowed-image (1) regressions pass: 35 total. cargo fmt and clippy with LLVM/allocation-audit and -D warnings pass. Runtime fixtures serialize ownership of the existing process-wide lazy-compiler callbacks.\n\nThis closes shared template retention, not TypeScript import/export integration. The test constructs TypedImport after parsing against the library signatures and removes every dependency definition from the client. Pure library fixtures omit the empty ts$main until TypeScript has module-qualified entry names. Remaining frontend module identity, initialization and export-interface work stays in 0c26889a7cd2c4060eb94ef93fd808c2857089dbf714735ca52b5d071bdb1564.","files":null}]}